WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:07.680
On the 1st of May 1960, a Soviet&nbsp;
S-75 Dvina surface-to-air missile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:13.200
smashed into an American U-2 spy plane&nbsp;
somewhere over the region of Sverdlovsk.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:19.740
Not wanting to lose face either in front of&nbsp;
the press or the Soviets, the US government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:26.640
concocted a cover story, claiming the plane&nbsp;
was a NASA research craft that went down after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:31.560
getting into difficulties. They really went to&nbsp;
town trying to make the deception as plausible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.760
as possible - explaining that the pilot had&nbsp;
radioed in a problem with his oxygen supply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:42.000
shortly before contact with the plane was lost.&nbsp;
They even went so far as to ground the US air&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:49.860
force's entire fleet of U-2s, ostensibly to&nbsp;
check the oxygen delivery systems for faults.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:55.500
It was an excellent piece of deception delivered&nbsp;
convincingly... but there was one small problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:01.800
Actually, there were *two* really fucking&nbsp;
massive problems. First, the plane's pilot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:09.240
Gary Powers, had not, as the Americans believed,&nbsp;
died in the crash. He'd ejected safely and was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:15.900
in the custody of the Soviets. Second, the U-2&nbsp;
spy plane, which was covered in more high tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:22.320
reconnaissance gear than a billionaire stalker,&nbsp;
had survived the missile strike with relatively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:28.560
minor damage, and the Soviets could see all&nbsp;
too clearly that this was no research craft.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:35.820
Seeing an opportunity to score a point against&nbsp;
their Cold War rivals, the Soviets wasted no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:40.860
time parading both the pilot and large chunks&nbsp;
of the spy plane in front of the world's press.

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:45.480
To make matters even worse, the two&nbsp;
superpowers had been due to meet for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:49.200
peace talks at a Paris summit just&nbsp;
two weeks after the plane was shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:55.080
down. With the Americans made to look like&nbsp;
mugs for planting such a public lie and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:59.640
Soviets furious that the Americans were&nbsp;
spying on them right before peace talks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:05.820
the summit was cancelled and the Cold War&nbsp;
continued unabated for the next 30 years.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:11.340
All in all, the whole thing was a bit&nbsp;
of a disaster for everyone involved.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:19.680
But there was one rather nice knock-on effect&nbsp;
- the 1960 U-2 incident directly led to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:25.140
development of what many people believe&nbsp;
is the greatest aeroplane ever built.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:30.000
That's a pretty bold claim, but if&nbsp;
you watch to the end of this video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.160
trust me - you're going to agree.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:39.600
This is the story of the Lockheed SR-71&nbsp;
Blackbird: the invincible spy plane.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:12.960
When the U-2 first flew in 1955, the&nbsp;
US government believed its lofty 70&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:18.120
thousand-foot cruising altitude put it&nbsp;
out of range of Soviet radar systems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:26.160
As Gary Powers proved in 1960, that&nbsp;
very much wasn't the case. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:31.920
Soviet radar technology was far more advanced than&nbsp;
the Americans had ever imagined, and that was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:38.460
serious problem. The Lockheed U-2 was relatively&nbsp;
slow with a large radar cross-section - if the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:44.940
Soviet's could successfully track it, it was&nbsp;
nothing more than a very expensive flying duck.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:51.180
The CIA invested serious cash trying&nbsp;
to make the U-2 less visible to radar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:55.740
but when those efforts failed it became&nbsp;
clear they were going to need a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.740 --> 00:05:01.740
plane entirely - one that would truly be&nbsp;
invulnerable to Soviet air defence systems.

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:09.180
Of course, building an invincible aeroplane was&nbsp;
impossible... but it just so happens that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:15.900
Americans had an aeronautical engineer who'd built&nbsp;
a career out of making the impossible possible.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:22.080
His name was Kelly Johnson, and to give you an&nbsp;
idea of the length and importance of his career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.400
I can tell you that he designed the&nbsp;
United's States first jet fighter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:31.020
the first military aircraft&nbsp;
to fly faster than 400 mph,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:38.340
the first Mach 2 capable fighter, and&nbsp;
the first aircraft ever to exceed Mach 3.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:44.460
Put simply, he is one of the most important&nbsp;
figures in the entire history of aviation.

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:50.340
At the time, he was head of Lockheed's Advanced&nbsp;
Development Projects, a department better known by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:57.180
its nickname, the Skunk Works. Johnson's team was&nbsp;
relatively small, but comprised some of the finest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:03.300
aerospace engineering minds not just in the US,&nbsp;
but the entire world. Together, Johnson and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:09.720
merry band of geniuses came up with a plane that&nbsp;
can only be described as a miracle of engineering.

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:17.580
The first iteration of that aircraft was known&nbsp;
as the A-12, and it was like nothing that had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:22.860
ever been built before. It was developed under&nbsp;
a cloak of absolute secrecy, and first took to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:27.480
the skies in the early 1960s when it was&nbsp;
tested at some place called Groom Lake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:35.220
though you may know it by another name: Area-51.&nbsp;
It was this plane - and others like it - that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:42.240
helped build Area-51's reputation as the home&nbsp;
of secret alien technology, and it isn't hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:47.820
to imagine why: the A-12 wouldn't have looked&nbsp;
out of place in a Ridley Scott space epic.

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:52.740
By the way, if the name 'A-12' sounds familiar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:57.300
you might be thinking of one of Elon&nbsp;
Musk's children, who's called...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:07.620
erm... uhh... yeah, no idea. The A-12 part of&nbsp;
the poor kid's... designation is a tribute to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:13.740
Musk's favourite plane, the SR-71, which&nbsp;
is ultimately what the A-12 became.
 &nbsp;

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:19.440
To begin with there were 3 different variants -&nbsp;
the regular A-12 was a straight-up reconnaissance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:25.260
plane, but there was also an interceptor -&nbsp;
the YF-12 - that was equipped with air-to-air&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:32.640
missiles and the particularly badass M-21,&nbsp;
which came with a detachable unmanned drone.

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:40.200
But none of those planes ever really, well,&nbsp;
took off, because waiting in the wings was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:48.120
the A-12's successor, the SR-71, nicknamed&nbsp;
Blackbird for its striking paint job. Although,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:53.100
little-known fact time - the paint was&nbsp;
actually an extremely dark shade of blue.

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:59.640
Capable of cruising 16 miles above the surface of&nbsp;
the earth at more than 3 times the speed of sound,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:06.420
the SR-71 was the perfect spy plane - it was one&nbsp;
of the first built with a 'stealth' design that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:12.420
dramatically reduced its radar signature,&nbsp;
and it flew so high and so fast that ground&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:19.740
fire couldn't touch it. By the time Soviet radar&nbsp;
systems locked on and SAM installations fired,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:28.620
it was simply too late - the SR-71 was long&nbsp;
gone. During three decades of active service,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:38.160
an estimated 4000 surface-to-air missiles were&nbsp;
fired at the SR-71. Not a single one hit its mark.

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:44.160
The Blackbird was such a revolutionary aircraft&nbsp;
that almost the entire thing had to be built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:49.140
from scratch using new technology. Air&nbsp;
temperatures at cruising altitude were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:56.580
in the region of -85 degrees celsius, but&nbsp;
the SR-71 flew so fast that friction with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.580 --> 00:09:03.000
the thin atmosphere generated temperatures of&nbsp;
beyond 300 degrees celsius across its frame.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:09.180
When a Blackbird landed after a flight&nbsp;
at Mach 3, its hull was so hot you could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:12.480
cook your dinner on it (although I'm&nbsp;
pretty sure that was frowned upon).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:20.460
Regular aviation aluminium would begin to soften&nbsp;
if exposed to such extreme heat, so the SR-71 was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:27.180
built almost entirely from a titanium alloy. That&nbsp;
was something that had never been done before, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:33.180
a whole new set of fabrication tools and methods&nbsp;
had to be invented before they could even start.

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:39.120
The incredible heat generated when the plane was&nbsp;
cruising at almost 3.5 times the speed of sound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:45.360
caused other problems, too - the airframe&nbsp;
expanded by almost a foot as it warmed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:52.260
so all the plane's panels had to be fitted with&nbsp;
plenty of play. That extra wiggle room meant the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:59.100
SR-71 steadily leaked fuel while on the ground,&nbsp;
because the fuel tanks didn't seal properly.

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:04.260
Incidentally, there happened to be a bit of&nbsp;
a shortage of titanium in the US at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:08.160
which was particularly unfortunate&nbsp;
because the vast majority of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:14.400
global supply came from the Soviet Union.&nbsp;
Buying direct clearly wasn't an option,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:20.940
so the CIA had to establish a number of dummy&nbsp;
corporations to get Skunk Works what they needed.

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:28.860
The first SR-71s went into service&nbsp;
in 1966 about 7 months before the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:33.960
England football team won the World&nbsp;
Cup (Which is completely irrelevant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:39.840
but it's always nice to mention&nbsp;
it). In total, just 32 were made.

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:44.760
These planes flew so high that SR-71 pilots&nbsp;
had to wear special pressure suits to prevent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:49.920
their blood from boiling in their veins if the&nbsp;
cockpit's integrity happened to be breached,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:56.400
and that incredible altitude meant the SR-71&nbsp;
could go where it pleased, though so far as we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:01.560
know none were ever actually flown over the Soviet&nbsp;
Union - by this point in the Cold War, tensions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:07.500
were simply too high to risk it. And besides,&nbsp;
the Blackbird could do a surprisingly good job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:13.140
at gathering reconnaissance even from outside&nbsp;
of Soviet airspace. The planes were equipped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:20.340
with side-facing cameras that could scan 100,000&nbsp;
square miles of enemy territory in a single hour.

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:28.080
For almost 30 years the SR-71 ruled the&nbsp;
skies, an untouchable phantom that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:33.120
occasionally coalesce on enemy radars&nbsp;
before disappearing into the ether.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:37.620
We'll likely never know exactly&nbsp;
which missions were flown and where,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.620 --> 00:11:45.840
but it's thought that SR-71s were used all over&nbsp;
the world and in almost every major war zone.

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:51.720
But by the 1990s, the use-case for this&nbsp;
most impressive machine was beginning to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:59.280
look a little shaky. For all its technological&nbsp;
brilliance, the SR-71 had one fatal flaw - it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:04.860
lacked a live data feed, meaning any intelligence&nbsp;
it gathered couldn't be analysed until the craft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.860 --> 00:12:11.880
was back at base. The existing planes could&nbsp;
always have been upgraded, but emerging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:17.940
technologies like spy satellites and unmanned&nbsp;
drones could do the same job more efficiently.

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:23.880
In the end, a combination of these new&nbsp;
technologies, politics, and the phenomenal running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:30.300
costs of the SR-71 saw it retired, though plenty&nbsp;
of senior figures in the air force considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:35.400
the move to be a grave mistake. After all, spy&nbsp;
satellites could be slow to get into position,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:41.040
and unmanned drone technology was still&nbsp;
in its infancy. But after briefly being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:47.700
brought out of retirement in 1999, the SR-71&nbsp;
was mothballed for good shortly afterwards.

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:55.140
The Blackbird is a relic from another era - a spy&nbsp;
plane built almost 60 years ago that's long since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.140 --> 00:13:03.600
been replaced by newer, more advanced models...&nbsp;
replaced, but never surpassed. To this day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:11.880
the SR-71 remains the fastest air-breathing&nbsp;
aeroplane ever built, and it still holds numerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:18.480
point-to-point records, including the fastest&nbsp;
flight from New York to London - in 1974 it made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:25.680
the journey in 1 hour 54 minutes, a full *hour*&nbsp;
faster than supersonic speed demon Concorde.

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:31.500
There’s something almost mythical about this&nbsp;
aeroplane. Those who built it talked about it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:37.740
in hushed tones, and the pilots who flew it&nbsp;
were simply in awe - in the words of US Air&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.740 --> 00:13:44.100
force Colonel Jim Watkins: At 85,000 feet and&nbsp;
Mach 3, it was almost a religious experience.

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:50.940
If you want to see an SR-71 for yourself today,&nbsp;
your only chance is to visit one of a select few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:56.460
museums around the world that were lucky enough&nbsp;
to get one. But here's the good news - a successor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.460 --> 00:14:05.040
is in the works. Commonly referred to as ‘Son of&nbsp;
Blackbird,’ the SR-72 could fly as early as 2025.

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:10.500
As for whether it will live up to the legacy&nbsp;
of its progenitor, only time will tell.

00:14:14.340 --> 00:14:15.420
Thanks for watching.

